s a central shape, opens the board vertically, and follows standard Othello conventions.\n\n**Evaluation:** All four valid moves (C4, D3, E6, F5) are strategically equivalent due to the initial board\'s perfect symmetry. F5 is chosen as it creates a solid, balanced opening board state with a vertical line of three friendly pieces, limiting the opponent to a restricted set of immediate responses (D6 and F4). It follows standard Reversi opening play.\n\n**Reason:** The board\'s symmetry makes the choices equal in objective outcome at this stage. F5 is a standard, generally favored opening move that adheres to principles of central control and restricted opponent mobility, flipping an opponent\'s piece to join a solid central formation.\n\n**Answer:** (F, 5)\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation: The current board state represents the standard opening position of Reversi. Due to the perfect symmetry of the board and the initial piece arrangement, all four valid moves—(C, 4), (D, 3), (E, 6), and (F, 5)—are strategically equivalent. Each move results in capturing one central piece and forming a line of three pieces of your color. They all leave the opponent with exactly two possible responses and maintain the game's balance without conceding any positional advantage. \n\nReason: Since all options are symmetric and theoretically equal, I have selected (F, 5). This is a conventional opening move that adheres to the principle of keeping pieces in the center (centralization). It creates a vertical line of three pieces (D5, E5, F5) and limits the opponent's immediate responses to predictable moves, setting a solid foundation for the early game.\n\nAnswer: (F, 5)"}
